Let's Become the Packet:
------------------------

    1) Suppose PC0 want to ping PC1:
    --------------------------------
        - PC0 192.168.10.10
        - Destination (192.168.20.20)
        - First PC0 checks: Is 192.168.20.20 is in my subnet: NO
        - So, It sends the packet to default gateway, PC0 --- --- (192.168.10.1 (R1))

    2) Router1 Receives the Packet:
    -------------------------------
        - The packet says: Destination IP: 192.168.20.20
        - Router 1 now looks into its Routing table
        - It asks: How do i reach 192.168.20.0/24

    3) What does Router-1 know:
    ---------------------------
        - Router 1 only knows the networks that are directly connected to it:
        - Which are (192.168.10.0/24), G0/0 and 10.10.10.0/30, G0/1
        - That's all, It does not know: 192.168.20.0/24
        - Because nobody has told Router-1 about that network yet
        - I don't know where 192.168.20.0/24 is, So it drops the packet

    4) This is Why Static Routing Exists: ****
    ------------------------------------------
        - Static Routing is simply telling the router:
        - If you want to reach the 192.168.20.0/24 network, send teh packet to Router 2 at 10.10.10.2"
        That's all static route does


        Verify through(Commands): ROUTER-1
        ----------------------------------
        Router1#sh ip route
        Codes: L - local, C - connected, S - static, R - RIP, M - mobile, B - BGP
               D - EIGRP, EX - EIGRP external, O - OSPF, IA - OSPF inter area
               N1 - OSPF NSSA external type 1, N2 - OSPF NSSA external type 2
               E1 - OSPF external type 1, E2 - OSPF external type 2, E - EGP
               i - IS-IS, L1 - IS-IS level-1, L2 - IS-IS level-2, ia - IS-IS inter area
               * - candidate default, U - per-user static route, o - ODR
               P - periodic downloaded static route

        Gateway of last resort is not set

             10.0.0.0/8 is variably subnetted, 2 subnets, 2 masks
        C       10.10.10.0/30 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/1
        L       10.10.10.1/32 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/1
             192.168.10.0/24 is variably subnetted, 2 subnets, 2 masks
        C       192.168.10.0/24 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/0
        L       192.168.10.1/32 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/0


        #########################################################
         1) there is no entry for 192.168.20.0/24,
         > That's exactly why Router-1 cannot forward the packet

         2) Same problem on Router-2
            (There is not entry for 192.168.10.0/24)

         4) So, Even if the packet somehow reached Router-2,
            the replay couldn't get back without a route

         ########################################################

        Router2#sh ip route
        Codes: L - local, C - connected, S - static, R - RIP, M - mobile, B - BGP
               D - EIGRP, EX - EIGRP external, O - OSPF, IA - OSPF inter area
               N1 - OSPF NSSA external type 1, N2 - OSPF NSSA external type 2
               E1 - OSPF external type 1, E2 - OSPF external type 2, E - EGP
               i - IS-IS, L1 - IS-IS level-1, L2 - IS-IS level-2, ia - IS-IS inter area
               * - candidate default, U - per-user static route, o - ODR
               P - periodic downloaded static route

        Gateway of last resort is not set

             10.0.0.0/8 is variably subnetted, 2 subnets, 2 masks
        C       10.10.10.0/30 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/1
        L       10.10.10.2/32 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/1
             192.168.20.0/24 is variably subnetted, 2 subnets, 2 masks
        C       192.168.20.0/24 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/0
        L       192.168.20.1/32 is directly connected, GigabitEthernet0/0

        Router2#

    5) What should we tell Router-1:
    --------------------------------
        - If you want to reach 192.168.20.0/24, send the packet to Router-2
        - Router-2 IPs, 10.10.10.2
        - Therefore the static route becomes:
            ip route 192.168.20.0 255.255.255.0 10.10.10.2

            ip route (create static route)
            192.168.20.0 (Destination Network)
            255.255.255.0 (Destination Subnet mask)
            10.10.10.2 (Net HOP)

    6) Same Route Router2 as well:
    ------------------------------
        Router-2 receives: Destination 192.168.10.10
        Router-2 checks: Do I know 192.168.10.0/24: NO
        So, We must also teach Router-2
        ip route 192.168.10.0 255.255.255.0 10.10.10.1
        Router-1 points to Router-2
        Router-2 points to Router-1
        This is why communication becomes bidirectional




























